+### Durations
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a string such as `1h30m` to a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A duration is one or more
+parts, each a number followed by a unit: `d`, `h`, `m`, `s` or `ms`. Units are lower case and must appear in that order,
+each at most once, as in `2d4h` or `1m500ms`. A bare number means seconds, so `30` is 30 seconds. An integer value, such
+as `default=30`, is also a number of seconds.
+
+If `min` or `max` is given, a value outside that range fails. Both bounds are inclusive. A bound that is omitted is
+unbounded. A `timedelta` value, such as a `default=timedelta(minutes=5)`, is accepted as-is, and a negative value fails.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    from datetime import timedelta
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--wait", type=click.Duration(min=timedelta(seconds=1), max=timedelta(hours=1)))
+    def cli(wait):
+        click.echo(wait.total_seconds())
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(cli, args=['--wait=90s'])
+    invoke(cli, args=['--wait=2h'])
+```
+
